Everything in skoro eventually becomes a burn `Tensor`. `skoro-backend` fixes *which* backends
are allowed and *what shape* each kind of tensor has. The shapes are worth learning precisely,
because every later page, from the derivative passes to the Jacobians, is phrased in them.

## Supported backends

`Backend` is a subtrait of burn's backend trait, sealed so that only the backends skoro has been
verified against implement it: `Flex` (CPU) and `CubeBackend` (GPU via cubecl). None of burn's
autodiff backends are among them. Skoro differentiates on its own (see
[Differentiable operations](../operations/)).

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-backend/src/lib.rs:17..32}
```

## Batches are independent problems

Axis 0 of every tensor indexes a **batch**. A batch of size $N$ is $N$ independent copies of the
same computation: row $b$ of the output depends only on row $b$ of every input. A model evaluated
at a batch of points returns a batch of log-densities, one per row, never their sum. Every map on
the following pages is therefore defined on one row, and extends to a batch row by row.

## Points, tangent vectors, and frames

A quantity lives on a manifold $\man$ (introduced on [Manifolds and retractions](../manifolds/)).
Two sizes describe it, and they differ whenever $\man$ is curved:

- its **storage size** $s$: how many numbers represent a point $x \in \man$;
- its **dimension** $d$: how many numbers represent a tangent vector $v \in \T_x\man$.

| Quantity | storage $s$ | dimension $d$ |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| `Real` | 1 | 1 |
| `Euclidean<3>` | 3 | 3 |
| `SO3` (a $3\times3$ rotation matrix) | 9 | 3 |
| `SE3` (a rotation and a translation) | 12 | 6 |
| `Tril(n)` (a packed lower triangle) | $n(n+1)/2$ | $n(n+1)/2$ |

The type aliases carry this distinction in their names:

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-backend/src/lib.rs:60..93}
```

| Alias | Shape | One row is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Point<B>` | `[batch, s]` | a point $x \in \man$ |
| `TangentVector<B>` | `[batch, d]` | a tangent vector $v \in \T_x\man \cong \R^d$ |
| `CotangentVector<B>` | `[batch, d]` | a cotangent (a row vector acting on $\T_x\man$) |
| `TangentFrame<B>` | `[batch, d, k]` | a $d \times k$ matrix $V = [v_1 \cdots v_k]$ of tangent vectors |
| `CotangentFrame<B>` | `[batch, d, k]` | a $d \times k$ matrix of cotangents, one per column |

A **frame** is the device that makes derivatives cheap: a linear map applied to a frame acts on all
$k$ columns at once, so pushing $k$ directions through a derivative costs one pass rather than $k$.
Axis 1 is the component axis and axis 2 indexes the columns. The Jacobian assemblers
([Jacobians](../jacobians/)) seed a frame with the columns of an identity matrix and read a whole
Jacobian out of one pass.

Points use storage size; tangent vectors, cotangents, gradients and Jacobian columns use dimension.
Confusing the two is the classic silent failure in this library: an `SE3` block sized 12 still
evaluates and still differentiates, but every column after the sixth means something else.

## Precision

The float policy is process-wide and locked on first use. See
[Backends and precision](../../guide/backends-and-precision/) and
[Precision is a lock-once policy](../../design/precision-policy/).

